The powerdomain of a neighbourhood system consists of the neighbourhoods that are finite unions of upper sets.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDmem`).** A powerdomain neighbourhood is a finite union of upper sets generated by neighbourhoods of the original system.

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.dagger`).** The dagger system is the upper-set power system associated with the original neighbourhood system.

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDmem_union`).** The union of two powerdomain neighbourhoods is a powerdomain neighbourhood.

*Proof.* Concatenate finite lists of generators; the resulting union is the union of the two original finite unions. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.dagger_isomorphic`).** Every neighbourhood system is domain-isomorphic to its dagger system.

*Proof.* This is the canonical isomorphism between a system and its upper-set power presentation. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.dagger_upSet_inter`).** If \(X,Y,Z\) are neighbourhoods and \(Z\subseteq X\cap Y\), then the intersection of the upper sets generated by \(X\) and \(Y\) is a dagger neighbourhood.

*Proof.* The intersection is the upper set generated by \(X\cap Y\), which is a neighbourhood because it contains the neighbourhood \(Z\). ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.mem_PDunion`).** A set belongs to the finite union generated by a list exactly when it belongs to the upper set generated by some member of the list.

*Proof.* This is the elementwise meaning of a union indexed first by the list and then by membership in it. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDmem_empty`).** The empty family is a powerdomain neighbourhood.

*Proof.* It is generated by the empty list. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDmem_iff_fin`).** A family is a powerdomain neighbourhood exactly when it is a union of a finite indexed family of upper sets generated by neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* Pass between a finite list and its indexing by the finite set of positions. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDmem_upSet`).** The upper set generated by any neighbourhood is a powerdomain neighbourhood.

*Proof.* Use the singleton list of generators. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDmem_master`).** The upper set generated by the master neighbourhood is a powerdomain neighbourhood.

*Proof.* Apply the singleton construction to the master neighbourhood. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.upSet_inter_nonempty_iff`).** Two generated upper sets have nonempty intersection exactly when some neighbourhood is contained in the intersection of their generators.

*Proof.* A member of both upper sets is precisely a neighbourhood contained in both generators. ∎
